<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. A second,
       earlier one would win in the served HTML (browsers and crawlers take the first). *@
    <meta name="description" content="Bit.Bmotion - a Blazor-native animation library inspired by Framer Motion: springs, gestures, variants, drag, layout and scroll animations, with all of the maths in C#." />

    @* What a link to the site looks like when it is shared. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.Bmotion" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Bmotion - a Blazor-native animation library" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Springs, gestures, layout animations and variants for Blazor. All of the maths in C#, none of the JavaScript wiring, and no animation library shipped alongside it." />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit (app.css, theme.js, site.js - shared by every Utilities demo), then this demo's
       own sample styles. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* The library's stylesheet. It plays the prerendered enter animations (BmPrerenderMode.Animate,
       set in AddDemoServices): without it an element prerendered that way is simply served at rest. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Bit.Bmotion/bit-bmotion.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.Bmotion.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    <ImportMap />
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" type="image/x-icon" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="512x512" href="icon-512.png" />
    @* Same render mode as the app itself, so <PageTitle> keeps working after hydration. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

@* data-enhance-nav="false": a link followed before the WebAssembly runtime has started is a plain
   page load rather than an enhanced one. Enhanced navigation fetches and merges the next page while
   the runtime is still starting, and the router the runtime then attaches can render the address
   it read before the merge - the URL says /getting-started and the page is the landing page. Once the
   app is interactive its router handles every link itself and this attribute is not consulted. *@
<body data-enhance-nav="false">
    @* Prerendered on the server, then hydrated by the WebAssembly runtime. The C# engine can't run
       during the prerender pass, so Bit.Bmotion (in BmPrerenderMode.Animate) serves each element at
       rest with its enter animation compiled to CSS - it plays as soon as the HTML arrives, and the
       runtime takes the element over where that animation got to. Gestures and everything else
       start once the runtime is interactive, which the shell marks with data-interactive (see
       Layout/MainLayout.razor). The library's script is not linked here: it is imported on demand
       the first time an animation runs. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />

    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>

    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
